// SIMULATED one week of vital signs for one fictional astronaut on the ISS.
// Day-by-day astronaut vitals are not public, so these are not real readings.
// Normal days follow what studies measured in space; event days are set just past
// the thresholds in vital-thresholds.json so every status (ok / warning / critical) appears.
//
// Normal-day values and where they come from:
//   Blood pressure ~110/70 mmHg: on the ISS 24-hour blood pressure is 8-10 mmHg lower than on Earth.
//     Norsk P et al. 2015, J Physiol. https://doi.org/10.1113/jphysiol.2014.284869
//   Sleep 6.0-6.5 h: ISS astronauts slept 6.09 h a night on average (SD 0.67).
//     Barger LK et al. 2014, Lancet Neurol. https://doi.org/10.1016/S1474-4422%2814%2970122-X
//   Heart rate 60-70 bpm, SpO2 97-98 %, temperature 36.7-37.1 °C: normal adult ranges.
//     https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/003399.htm
//     https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/pulse-oximetry/
//     https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/001982.htm
//   Mood (1-5): team demo scale, no study.
//
// Event days:
//   2026-11-09: heart rate 54 (athlete range), SpO2 93 %, short sleep -> warnings
//   2026-11-10: acute illness scenario -> every indicator critical
//   2026-11-14: today, heart rate 105, SpO2 89 %, 5 h sleep -> warnings + critical sleep
